{"name":"Red Oxide","hex":"#5d1f1e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red oxide","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["ferric oxide (Fe2O3)","A brownish red colour, often of paint."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_oxide"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iron(III) oxide","blocks":[{"text":"Iron(III) oxide or ferric oxide is the inorganic compound with the formula Fe2O3. It occurs in nature as the mineral hematite, which serves as the primary source of iron for the steel industry. It is also known as red iron oxide, especially when used in pigments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Haematite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png/330px-Haematite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron(III)_oxide","wikidata":"Q419170"},{"title":"Oxide","blocks":[{"text":"An oxide is a chemical compound containing at least one oxygen atom and one other element in its chemical formula. \"Oxide\" itself is the dianion of oxygen, an O2− ion with oxygen in the oxidation state of −2. Most of the Earth's crust consists of oxides. Even materials considered pure elements often develop an oxide coating. For example, aluminium foil develops a thin skin of Al2O3 that protects the foil from further oxidation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rutile-unit-cell-3D-balls.png/330px-Rutile-unit-cell-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxide","wikidata":"Q50690"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/500px-Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/1280px-Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/500px-Iron%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sample of iron(III) oxide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIron(III)-oxide-sample.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA.jpg/500px-%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA.jpg/1280px-%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA.jpg/500px-%D0%96%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D1%83%